#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564547} :activity/id #uuid "48a27db3-de33-52fc-a27c-3fb1cd88a0ab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12262de4-ff35-5b6d-902b-cc063519daf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563994} {:answer/id #uuid "26e486e8-edca-5347-8954-f90b314bfea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564026} {:answer/id #uuid "3108d9ea-bf52-50bb-b021-c32e5e1cac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564052} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564207} {:answer/id #uuid "f422fa0c-c176-5bb8-b7d5-4e0f06f500ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564207} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df7676f-854b-544e-891a-356915ca7afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37587" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} {:answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510890} {:answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511041} {:answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511062} {:answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y &=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564000} {:answer/id #uuid "8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564164} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} {:answer/id #uuid "c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564274} {:answer/id #uuid "d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81042" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564127} {:answer/id #uuid "ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564230} {:answer/id #uuid "c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564275} {:answer/id #uuid "d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} {:answer/id #uuid "f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :question/math-academy-id "q-105286" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c9d9f0-2dbe-5ac0-8036-c9df0b172d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563977} {:answer/id #uuid "28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564031} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf80967-03e1-5b5a-9f33-6ed0d8781d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564161} {:answer/id #uuid "c76ffc80-3225-5c67-acc7-073ef6b43896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564279} {:answer/id #uuid "f191d36c-e930-5f0a-8a04-f0c1f06aec36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564031} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f466cf-09b5-5527-b854-f04096edcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104974" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564564}}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563961}]}
